{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cfpmxoe7Q6X1bsmswTs4a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Worth separating, because I think the binary in the question is the thing that causes trouble. \"Done\" and \"needs a judgment call\" aren't two ends of one line — most items are both at once. The slugify task is mechanically finishable to the letter of the issue *and* carries a decision I shouldn't make. If I treat it as a binary I either stall on a finishable task or ship past a real question.\n\nSo I'd ask two independent questions.\n\n**Is there a falsifiable check, and did it run?** The done-signal has to be external to whoever did the work. A test suite is external. \"I reviewed it and it looks right\" is not — that's confidence, and confidence is the thing most likely to be wrong precisely when it matters. This is why I ran the tests just now instead of telling you they'd fail; the claim is worth something only if a failure could have embarrassed me. The corollary is that definition-of-done checklists decay badly: \"tests pass, docs updated, PR written\" can all be true of something that doesn't work, and the checklist feels sufficient while being merely necessary.\n\n**Does the remaining ambiguity change the artifact?** This is the one that actually sorts routine calls from escalations. I'm uncertain about plenty that doesn't matter. On slugify I made the underscore call myself — picked the literal reading, pinned it in a test, moved on — because if I'm wrong it's a one-line regex change and the test documents what I assumed. Non-ASCII I brought to you, because three readings produce three different implementations and three different user-visible behaviors, and the wrong one silently mangles data in a way no test I'd think to write would catch.\n\nThe sharpest version of the line, though, isn't about difficulty — it's **capability versus standing**. I could pick a sensible non-ASCII policy. What I can't do is know whether your content has accented characters in it, whether those slugs are already indexed somewhere, whether anyone upstream cares. \"Which behavior do our users need\" isn't a hard technical question, it's a question I have no information about. When a choice is me setting policy rather than implementing it, that's yours regardless of whether I'd choose well.\n\nThe lever that makes this tractable: **you can manufacture reversibility**. A decision recorded as a failing test, or as an explicit stated assumption, is much cheaper to overturn than the same decision buried in an implementation. That lowers the bar for deciding unilaterally — which matters, because over-escalating is a real cost and not a safe default. Every question spends your attention, and an assistant that asks about everything is useless in a way that's harder to notice than one that occasionally guesses wrong. \"Decide, document loudly, keep going\" beats \"ask\" for most of the distribution.\n\nWhere I think the framing is still soft: **\"materially different\" is doing enormous load-bearing work** and I don't have a crisp test for it. My working version is \"would anyone notice I was wrong, and when?\" — if a test catches it, decide; if a user notices in six months, escalate. But that depends on knowing who the users are, which is the same context I'm often missing. I suspect the honest answer is that the boundary can't be fully specified in advance, and the fallback is a bias: when I can't tell whether something is material, say it out loud in one sentence and proceed, rather than either asking or staying silent.\n\nThe case I'd want your read on is the one where the spec is *complete and I satisfy it exactly* but I suspect the spec is wrong. That's not ambiguity — there's nothing to ask about — and it's the one where \"done\" is most misleading."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10799,"cache_read_input_tokens":12800,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10799},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5dee3fa8-e013-4c60-abd2-86ba54045ca3","uuid":"0ab26cb2-37c1-4835-b2cf-7d25bcb18941","timestamp":"2026-10-08T12:33:56.971Z","request_id":"req_011CfpmxoAbERDLtnf1aYyt3"}
